ly  well first of all we saw in the recent  spending review the 5p cut to fuel due  to it was the biggest cut in in many  years but i recognize that's been more  than outweighed by the pressure on the  wholesale cost of energy that's why we  have to help people directly we have to  make sure that the kind of support  through the 37 billion pound package  that the chancellor has announced going  getting money directly to people through  their energy bills through council tax  through changes to universal credit  through the direct support that we've  announced can make a difference and will  make a difference but this is an  enormous challenge and i recognize i  have constituents who are profoundly  affected by the cost of energy i've  always campaigned both as a backbencher  and a minister to try and keep fuel duty  uh down to to to reduce the impact of  that and i think the chancellor will  continue to have to look at all the  tools at his disposal uh to try and keep  these costs down because of course it is  the case that when fuel duty all fuel  prices rise substantially it has a  broader impact on the cost of living  it certainly does i mean the one thing  that could be done right now is to  reduce fuel duty again couldn't it  well i think that's obviously a question  for the treasury i i i i  there's always a strong case for looking  at whatever we can do to help on these  things um but but let's say the most  recent fiscal event we had the biggest  cut in fuel duty that's ever been  delivered um by a government that is a  substantial move  and and  and of course you know i think it's  right that we're looking to help people  in other ways 37 billion pounds is a  very substantial package of support it  does mean that uh some of the poorest  families in society will be getting up  to uh 1200 pounds uh to to to help that  is a substantial difference and we've  got to continue to look to see where we  can put our arms around people and make  that kind of difference  now there's some reporting this morning  that people who work in health and  social care teachers as well are finding  it impossible to fill their tanks and  get to work because of the cost of fuel  what should be done to try and help  those people who work in the public  sector  well clearly where people are  recompensed for their travel that needs  to be looked at though those figures  need to be looked at to make sure that  they're keeping pace um with the prices  um but also we need to make sure um that  we are you know making sure that people  actually do have keep more of the money  that they earn and that's why i think  the rising of the threshold in national  insurance is really important that will  keep more money uh in people's pockets  uh it's why the whole range of measures  the 37 billion pound package of support  that the chancellor has announced can  make a real difference to keeping people  going uh supporting people but it's also  why and i think the government has been  clear about this we need to keep all of  these things under review to see if more  help is needed  when will that help be coming though  because we we do keep hearing that more  help may well be needed and things are  under review we've seen some measures  come in but as you'll know that those  measures have been criticized as not  being enough  well i think we we have uh you know had  a range of measures some of which have  already come in 150 pound uh council tax  rebate increases in the household  support fund and the the increase in the  the national living wage and the cut  field gc they're already in place uh the  next step will be the increase in  national insurance thresholds which will  make more money uh to what people uh can  can keep from their pay packets uh and  then eight million households on mean  tested benefits will be directly sent  the first installment of their 650 pound  cost of living payment um so these  things are rolling in um over time i i  think they 