nister so i've listened to this  no i do not think so  um  i don't uh  i wish he could be but he isn't um and  that is a bit sad i like boris i don't  think he's a bad guy you know um but i  don't  i don't know enough about what he's got  up to but i do not think that he's a  good role model for children  apologies for setting you up like that  mr walker that question was put to uh  miss burble sing is boris johnson a good  role model you heard her answer he's not  well that's her opinion of course she's  entitled to her opinion on that i think  actually anyone who leads this country  is a role model someone that children  can look up to the prime minister has  shown phenomenal resilience through the  coveted pandemic and  leadership and and i think that's  something  that children should learn about  understand about in schools i think it's  also he's a great communicator and one  of the things we seek to teach uh in all  our schools is the importance of  communication  literacy through great speech and  language  and so there's plenty for people to look  up to in that respect and i think the  prime minister will be judged in the  long run uh by his legacy uh the way in  which he's seen uh this country uh  through the pandemic and delivered some  of the strongest group for this country  the way we've invested um that's  something that i i  think i'm very proud to support him  yeah i mean legacy of breaking the law  as you'll know has been fined for um  those lock down parties i mean it's  embarrassing isn't it you said that you  think he he's a great communicator but  it's embarrassing that the school social  media  doesn't think that the prime minister is  a good role model  i think the prime minister has accepted  that there are things for which he's uh  apologized and it's quite right but she  should do that and in that respect as  well that's something that i think it's  important that children understand the  importance of acknowledging when you've  got things uh wrong but i think he's  also someone who has taken the big calls  taking great uh decisions in the long  run and and  and i think that's why uh the party uh  have been behind him when he's had this  vote of confidence earlier in the week  that's why i think he needs to now  continue to show that he's listening for  british people and delivering on their  priorities  and tell us there mr walker about this  announcement today over 14 000 extra  school places um being uh assigned how  is that going to be paid for  uh so this is um  an exciting announcement but we are  delivering not just in the the  mainstream school sector where we are  going to be delivering uh more uh  targeted places in the areas where  they're needed most new school  provisions particularly in the 16 to 19  sector in areas that don't have it in  our  education investment areas that we set  out during the white paper but also  crucially the specialist places which i  know the system has been crying out for  and new specialists and alternative  provision settings in terms of how  that's being paid for the chancellor set  out as a spending review 2.6 billion  pounds um for the specialist education  sector this is one element of how that  money will be spent so  new provision where it's needed and i  know from listening to schools and  listening to teachers in my constituency  there are calls for this up and down the  country and  meeting that need we're also going to be  investing in specialist settings in  mainstream schools and expansion for  existing specialist settings this is one  element of that 2.6 billion package  and alongside that the investment in  mainstream schools targeted to be areas  that need the most is part of the  ongoing investment by the department uh  to deliver new school places in a way  that will help to level up education and  meet our ambitions to drive up literacy  and numeracy and attainment in every  area of the country  and with that in mind mr walker why has  ther